<title>DENMARK: Danish Dec North Sea oil production rises 1.2 pct.</title>
<headline>Danish Dec North Sea oil production rises 1.2 pct.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1997-01-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>Danish Underground Consortium (DUC) oil production from its North Sea fields rose 1.2 percent in December 1996 from November of the same year, operator A.P. Moeller Group said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>It told Reuters that oil production was 980,000 tonnes in December or 232,077 barrels per day (bpd), compared with 229,238 bpd in November.</p>
<p>DUC is responsible for virtually the entire output from Denmark's North Sea oil and gas fields. Denmark is the third-biggest producer in western Europe after Norway and Britain.</p>
<p>Production data (figures in 1,000 bpd):</p>
<p>Field			Dec 96	 Nov 96	  Dec 95 </p>
<p>Dan			  70.3	   68.0	    63.9 </p>
<p>Kraka			 7.3	    4.4	     7.0 </p>
<p>Regnar			0.0	    0.0	     1.1 </p>
<p>Gorm			 48.9	   50.2	    39.4 </p>
<p>Skjold		     34.5	   36.7	    35.3 </p>
<p>Rolf			  3.5	    3.3	     4.5 </p>
<p>Dagmar			0.4	    0.3	     0.3 </p>
<p>Tyra			 24.2	   23.8	    29.7 </p>
<p>Valdemar		    3.5	    2.8	     1.0 </p>
<p>Roar			  8.0	    8.2		-  </p>
<p>Svend			31.5	   31.5		-  </p>
<p>Total A.P. Moeller Group owns 39 percent of DUC. Its two partners, Royal Dutch/Shell group and Texaco Inc own 46 percent and 15 percent respectively.</p>
<p>-- Chris Follett,Copenhagen newsroom, +45-33-969652</p>
